(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-cardiac-pathophysiology-quiz"]={"title":"Cardiac Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What causes most acute pericarditis?","choices":["Most follow myocardial infarction","Most are idiopathic","Most follow cardiac surgery","Most are bacterial"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The two statements sit together: unexplained in the sense that nothing is identified, but viral in most of those cases. Most of those are viral. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein carries the highest risk, and why?","choices":["High density lipoprotein, because it is high in cholesterol","Low density lipoprotein, because it is high in cholesterol","Very low density lipoprotein, because it is high in cholesterol","All lipoproteins carry equal risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The composition is what determines the risk, which is why the lipoproteins are not interchangeable even though all of them carry lipid. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which are given as uncommon causes of coronary heart disease?","choices":["Coronary vasospasm and thrombosis","Familial hypercholesterolemia and high fat diet","Reduced oxygen content of the blood, and poor perfusion","Rheumatic fever and infective endocarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both reach the same endpoint from outside the coronary artery: the vessel may be open, but what flows through it either carries too little oxygen or arrives at too low a pressure. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why is unstable angina included among the acute coronary syndromes?","choices":["It always involves complete vessel occlusion","It is caused by vasospasm rather than plaque","It may progress to acute ischemia","It occurs only in patients with prior infarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is grouped by where it is heading rather than by where it currently is, which is the point of treating it as acute. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How are the coronary syndromes classified?","choices":["By which coronary artery is involved","By the severity and onset of cardiac symptoms","By the lipoprotein pattern of the patient","By the degree of luminal occlusion alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The division is clinical rather than anatomical, which is why two syndromes with similar plaques sit in different categories. Into chronic and acute. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What happens to the valves as rheumatic endocardial inflammation progresses?","choices":["Immediate calcification of the leaflets","Swelling and erosion first","Ballooning of the leaflets into the atrium","Rupture of the papillary muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The scarring is what remains decades later, and it is why rheumatic fever appears as a cause of stenosis. Then scarring and shortening of the valvular structures. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is a prerequisite for infective endocarditis?","choices":["Pre-existing calcification of the valve","Invasion of the bloodstream by the organism","A recent myocardial infarction","Immune cross-reactivity with streptococcal antigens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The valve is reached through the blood, so bacteremia has to come first. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is a vegetation?","choices":["Microorganisms enmeshed in fibrin deposits","A calcified nodule on the valve annulus","A ballooned segment of valve leaflet","A thrombus within the left atrial appendage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As they enlarge they interfere with valve function and predispose to embolism, so the lesion does harm in two distinct ways. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Why does the heart compensate well for a slowly progressive stenosis?","choices":["It hypertrophies its myocardial cells as the gradient develops","It dilates the affected chamber immediately","It develops collateral circulation across the valve","It reduces the heart rate permanently"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Slow progression allows the adaptation, which is why such valves can be severely narrowed before anything is noticed. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"By what mechanism does rheumatic fever damage the heart?","choices":["An immune attack arising from cross-reactivity after streptococcal infection","Direct invasion of the valve leaflets by the organism","Calcium deposition on the leaflets","Ischemic necrosis of the papillary muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The organism is long gone; what damages the heart is the immune response aimed at something that resembles it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What happens once the injured endothelium becomes more permeable?","choices":["Calcium is deposited directly into the media","Leukocytes are recruited and low density lipoprotein leaks through to be oxidized","Smooth muscle cells migrate out of the vessel wall","The vessel dilates to preserve flow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lipid has to get into the wall before it can do harm, and the increased permeability is what lets it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Where are nearly all myocardial infarcts located, and which artery is most often responsible?","choices":["The right ventricle, most often the right coronary artery","The left ventricular walls, most often the left anterior descending","The atria, most often the circumflex","The interventricular septum only, most often the circumflex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The left ventricle does the pressure work, so it is the chamber whose supply matters most, and one artery accounts for the largest share. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What happens in mitral regurgitation?","choices":["Blood flows from atrium to ventricle during systole","Blood leaks from the aorta into the ventricle during diastole","Flow across the valve is obstructed during diastole","Blood flows back from ventricle to atrium during systole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and because the leak occurs during systole, part of every beat is wasted going the wrong way. Raising left atrial volume and pressure. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which factors are associated with dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Alcohol, genetics, pregnancy and a post-viral state","Amyloidosis","Aortic valve calcification","Streptococcal cross-reactivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Four causes with nothing obvious in common, which is why the condition is grouped by what the ventricle does rather than by what caused it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Fibrous encasement of the heart developing over years","Adhesion of the heart to surrounding mediastinal structures","Failure of the ventricle to contract because of inflammation","External compression of the chambers by pericardial fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The heart is prevented from filling from outside, so the muscle and valves may be entirely normal while the output collapses. Impairing filling. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What do the macrophages within a plaque release, and with what effect?","choices":["Collagen and fibrin, which stabilize the plaque","Enzymes that clear the lipid core entirely","Inflammatory mediators and growth factor","Vasodilators that increase flow through the vessel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the step that makes the lesion self-sustaining: the cells that arrived to deal with the lipid recruit more cells and more wall growth. Attracting more leukocytes and stimulating smooth muscle proliferation. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of stable, typical angina?","choices":["Vasospasm occurring unpredictably at rest","Plaque rupture with acute thrombosis","Stenotic vessels reduce flow to a critical level","Inflammation of the pericardium limiting filling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The narrowing is fixed, so it is the rise in demand that precipitates each episode -- which is why the attacks are predictable. So perfusion fails under increased workload. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the significance of a large lipid core within a plaque?","choices":["A large core makes the plaque more stable","A large core prevents platelet aggregation","A large core indicates the plaque has been cleared","Plaques with large lipid cores are fragile and rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Size and stability run in opposite directions, which is why the dangerous plaque is not necessarily the one narrowing the lumen most. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does valvular stenosis change the work the heart does?","choices":["It imposes extra VOLUME work, since blood returns across the valve","It reduces the work of the heart by limiting flow","It imposes extra PRESSURE work","It has no hemodynamic effect until the valve is completely closed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the distinction that organizes the whole valve section, and it predicts which chamber hypertrophies. Since blood must be forced through a smaller opening. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How do the capillaries within the atherosclerotic arterial wall contribute to disruption?","choices":["They deliver macrophages that dissolve the fibrous cap","They drain lipid out of the core, collapsing the plaque","Their thin walls bleed","They constrict, producing local ischemia within the wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The plaque can be disrupted from the inside as well as from the lumen, which is a route easy to overlook. Depositing blood products and raising pressure within the plaque. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Why does aortic stenosis predispose the heart to ischemia and angina?","choices":["The stenotic valve obstructs the coronary ostia directly","Hypertrophy and high ventricular pressures raise demand beyond what supply can meet","The ventricle dilates and thins, reducing perfusion","Calcium emboli lodge in the coronary arteries"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angina can arise with entirely normal coronary arteries, because the problem is on the demand side of the same equation. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What are the primary causes of valvular stenosis?","choices":["Post-inflammatory scarring from rheumatic fever","Infective endocarditis and papillary muscle rupture","Homocysteine-induced vascular injury","Coxsackievirus infection of the valve leaflets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One is an immune injury from decades earlier and the other is simple wear, which is why the two produce stenosis in very different age groups. Valvular calcification with aging. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What defines dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["A thickened, hyperkinetic ventricular muscle mass","A stiff, fibrotic ventricle with impaired filling","Cardiac failure with dilation of one or both ventricular chambers","Inflammation with leukocyte infiltration and necrosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is named for the chamber change, and the causes listed for it are otherwise unrelated to one another. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What properties make homocysteine harmful to vessels?","choices":["It is atherogenic but protects against thrombosis","It raises low density lipoprotein production in the liver","It inhibits smooth muscle proliferation","It is atherogenic and prothrombotic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two harms at once: it damages the wall and it promotes clot on that damaged wall, which is the same one-two that drives coronary events generally. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why is a patient with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy symptomatic?","choices":["Reduced contractility of a thin, dilated ventricle","Fibrosis restricting filling","Backflow across an incompetent aortic valve","Ventricular outflow obstruction and impaired diastolic filling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The thickened muscle obstructs the way out and stiffens the chamber, so both halves of the cycle suffer. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which determinants of workload increase myocardial oxygen demand?","choices":["Heart rate, preload, afterload and contractility","Coronary perfusion pressure and collateral circulation","Plaque size and composition","Serum lipoprotein concentration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Raising any one of the four raises demand, which is why ischemia can appear with the coronary anatomy entirely unchanged. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"At what degree of occlusion is blood flow described as compromised?","choices":["Twenty-five percent","Fifty percent","Ninety-five percent","Seventy-five percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It takes a great deal of narrowing before flow suffers, which is why plaques grow silently for years. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What produces adhesive mediastinopericarditis?","choices":["Suppurative or caseous pericarditis","Fluid accumulating without inflammation","A dense, non-elastic sac encasing the heart","Immune cross-reactivity after streptococcal infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cardiac workload rises because the heart is now working against the structures it is stuck to. Or surgery, destroying the sac so the heart adheres to surrounding structures. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the immediate metabolic consequence of reduced oxygen supply to cardiac cells?","choices":["The formation of adenosine triphosphate falls","Lactate is converted back into glucose within the cell","Calcium is actively pumped out of the cell more rapidly","Oxygen extraction from the blood falls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cell loses its energy currency first, and everything that follows -- failure to contract, then death -- is downstream of that. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What distinguishes very low density lipoprotein from low density lipoprotein?","choices":["It is high in cholesterol and decreases risk","It carries cholesterol back to the liver","It is not associated with atherosclerotic risk","It is high in triglycerides rather than cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both raise risk, so the distinction to hold is what each is carrying rather than which direction it pushes risk. Increases risk. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
